# 19 Solid bodies: embedded boundaries and cut cells {#ch:embed}

## Volume and face fractions

A solid body is not meshed. It cuts through the Cartesian cells, and
`embed.h` describes it with two fields (figure 19.1):

- $c_s$ (`cs`): the fraction of each cell's volume that is fluid;

- $f_s$ (`fs`): the fraction of each face's area that is fluid.

`solid(cs, fs, phi)` computes both from a level-set function $\phi$
that is positive in the fluid, in exactly the way `fraction()` computes
$f$. In the `metric` event `embed.h` sets $c_m = c_s$ and
$f_m = f_s$, so every operator of the previous chapters (divergence, face
velocity, Poisson coefficients, VOF update, CFL) is weighted by the open
volume and open face area. That substitution turns the whole solver into a
finite-volume cut-cell method.

