## Finite volumes in a cut cell

For a cut cell the conservation law [(7.1)](07_cfd_concepts) has one more
surface: the piece of solid wall $\Gamma_b$ inside the cell. For a diffusion
or Poisson operator,
$$
\int_{\text{cell}}\nabla\cdot(\alpha\nabla s)\,dV =
\sum_{\text{faces}} f_s\,\Delta^{d-1}\,\alpha\,\partial_n s
\;+\; \int_{\Gamma_b}\alpha\,\partial_n s\,dA.
$$
The face terms are the usual ones multiplied by $f_s$. The wall term
depends on the boundary condition:

- homogeneous Neumann (for example pressure, by default): the wall flux
  is zero and nothing is added;

- Dirichlet (for example $\mathbf{u}= 0$ in the viscous solve): the wall flux
  needs the normal gradient of $s$ at the wall, computed as below.

`embed_flux()` returns this wall flux, and `poisson.h` and
`viscosity-embed.h` add it to the residual and to the relaxation
(the `embed_flux` branch in `relax()`).

## Gradient at the wall for a Dirichlet condition {#sec:embed-dirichlet}

Following Johansen and Colella \[[6](references)\]: from the centroid of the
wall segment, draw the normal into the fluid (figure 19.2). It crosses the
next two grid lines (columns of cell centres in the direction where $|n|$ is
largest) at distances $d_0$ and $d_1$. Interpolate $s$ at those crossings,
$v_0$ and $v_1$, with quadratic interpolation along the grid line. A
quadratic through $(0, s_b)$, $(d_0, v_0)$, $(d_1, v_1)$ has slope at the
wall
$$
\partial_n s\big|_b = \dfrac{1}{\Delta}\,\dfrac{d_1\dfrac{v_0 - s_b}{d_0} - d_0\dfrac{v_1 - s_b}{d_1}}{d_1 - d_0},
$$
which is a third-order estimate. If the second point is not available the
code uses the second-order $(v_0 - s_b)/(d_0\Delta)$, and in degenerate cases
it uses the cell centre value.

