## Boundary conditions on the embedded surface

Conditions on the solid are set like domain conditions, with the side name
`embed`:

~~~literatec
u.n[embed] = dirichlet(0.);  u.t[embed] = dirichlet(0.);  u.r[embed] = dirichlet(0.);
~~~

`embed.h` redefines `dirichlet()` and `neumann()` so that,
called on the embedded boundary, they return the wall value (and mark it as
Dirichlet) or the wall gradient, instead of a ghost value. The default for
the pressure is a zero normal gradient, consistent with the wall-normal
velocity being zero.

## The small-cell problem

The CFL condition in a cut cell is $\Delta t< c_s\Delta/(f_s|u|)$: a cell with
$c_s = 10^{-3}$ would force a step a thousand times smaller. Basilisk does
not reduce the step. For tracers, `update_tracer()` uses *flux
redistribution*: a cut cell is advanced with the full flux only if it can
absorb it; otherwise it is advanced up to its own limit
$\Delta t_{\max} = c_s\Delta/\max|u_f|$ and the excess
$(\Delta t- \Delta t_{\max})F$ is shared among its neighbours in proportion to
$c_s^2$. The total amount is conserved.

The pressure equation has a related difficulty. In a cell with tiny $c_s$
the Poisson coefficients are tiny, the cell is weakly connected to its
neighbours, and a pressure difference across it produces a large velocity
when divided by its small volume. `fractions_cleanup()` removes cells
whose volume and face fractions are inconsistent (for example a cell with
$c_s > 0$ but all faces closed).

## Force on the body {#sec:embed-force}

The force exerted by the fluid on the solid is the integral of the stress
over the wall:
$$
\mathbf{F} = \oint_{\Gamma_b}\left(-p\,\mathbf{I} + 2\mu\mathbf{D}\right)\cdot\mathbf{n}_{\text{out}}\,dA,
$$
with $\mathbf{n}_{\text{out}}$ pointing from the solid into the fluid. In a
cut-cell code this becomes a sum over cut cells of the facet area times the
stress at the facet centroid.

In 2D, `embed_force(p, u, mu, &Fp, &Fmu)` returns the pressure and
viscous parts (the Kármán example in [chapter 5](05_examples) uses it for the drag
coefficient). Its viscous part asserts `dimension == 2`, so in 3D the sum
has to be written out in a `foreach()` loop over cells with
$0 < c_s < 1$:

- `embed_geometry(point, &b, &n)` returns the facet area (as a
  fraction of $\Delta^{d-1}$), its centroid `b` in cell units and the unit
  normal `n`, which points *into* the solid, so the pressure
  contribution of a facet of area $A$ is $+p\,\mathbf{n}A$;

- the pressure at the centroid is interpolated from the cell and its
  fluid neighbours;

- the viscous traction on a no-slip wall is
  $\mathbf{F}_\mu = -\sum\mu\,[\mathbf{g} + \mathbf{n}(\mathbf{g}\cdot\mathbf{n})]\,A$ with
  $\mathbf{g} = \nabla\mathbf{u}\cdot\mathbf{n}$ from `embed_gradient()`.

With `reduced.h` the solver pressure is the dynamic pressure $p'$, so
the hydrostatic part $\rho\,\mathbf{G}\cdot\mathbf{x}$ must be added back before
integrating. A useful check of sign and magnitude is a fully submerged body
in still water, which must feel its buoyancy $\rho g V$.

